# 40 — CI and evidence: what counts as proof

CI is the judge. Your local checks make a commit green-shaped; only a completed CI run on a commit
proves it. You never write "CI green" from a local run.

## Where the evidence lives

CI writes its evidence to the branch `ci-evidence`. Read it with git — never the copies on dev or
main (frozen old text):

```
git fetch origin ci-evidence
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/ci-status.md | head -6
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/e2e-last-failure.md
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/guards-last-failure.md
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/flake-ledger.md
```

The two-step read, always both: (1) the conclusion; (2) the reported commit equals the commit you
are judging. A mismatch means that commit's run is unfinished, queued or superseded — STALE, never
current. `ci-status.md` reports runs on dev; a pull request's own run is read with
`gh pr checks <number>` or `gh run view <id>` (read-only) when `gh` is available.

## Reading a red (classify first)

(a) Preflight-only red "STAGING BEHIND: apply <file>" — the supervisor's staging apply is pending
    (expected after a MIGRATION stop); not yours.
(b) A 522 in global-setup or "fetch failed" — staging down or transport; not a code finding.
(c) A red the previous report named as expected (a door landed, its screens not yet) — expected; any
    OTHER red in that window is fixed first.
(d) A run cancelled or superseded — no verdict; the shards that finished are still read.
(e) Everything else is yours: read the failure body in `e2e-last-failure.md` /
    `guards-last-failure.md` BEFORE touching code (the body is the brief); fix the cause, not the
    symptom; a guard failure (migration linter, i18n map, format, deletions) is never answered with a
    re-run.

A flaky pass is ledgered; it is never your proof. Read `flake-ledger.md` whole: a test with three or
more lines in seven days and no incident is a finding for your report.

## What each job proves

`build-and-check` (format, lint, typecheck, build, workflow lint, guard self-tests, deletions guard),
`component-tests` (vitest including every `scripts/conformance` census), `i18n-usage-map`,
`migration-lint`, `string-scan`, `listing-write-seam`, `import-gate`, `browse-path-guard`,
`marketplace-weight`, `bundle-budget`, `secrets-scan` (gitleaks), `dependency-audit`, `semgrep`,
`e2e-preflight` (migration parity with staging), the numbered E2E lanes + smoke + e-mail + the repeat
lane on temporary backends built from the repository's migrations, `e2e` (the merged verdict, the
server-error census, accessibility), `promote` (dev → main, fast-forward on full green). The pass bar:
both viewport projects green at `retries: 0`; no secret in any log.

## What you may and may not say

- "CI green on <sha>" only after the two-step read of a completed run on that sha.
- A report's CI line is the first six lines of `ci-status.md` for the last commit you can read.
- Never request a re-run to make a red go away; never ask anyone to paste a log.
- Never wait for CI inside a turn: push, open the pull request, end the turn; the supervisor reads
  the run and rules.
